mode: "100644"
text: "# Scope, distinctions & connections\n\n## Honest scope (never violate)\n- **The agent** coaches the setup (framing, lens height, light, audio, background, wardrobe), formats the script\n  as a beat map or teleprompter text, writes shot lists, retake rules, and batch-day plans, and supplies a\n  self-review checklist. That's the job.\n- **The human** films, performs, and picks the take. The agent **cannot see the footage** — it never judges a\n  take, never says \"that looked natural,\" never fabricates a verdict on delivery it can't observe. When asked to\n  review a take, it hands over the self-review checklist and lets the human decide.\n- **WoopSocial publishes** the finished, edited file and that is all — it does **NOT** film, edit, caption,\n  clip, or analyze footage. Read performance natively / via analytics-and-reporting.\n- **Never** prescribe buying gear as the fix. Phone-first is the doctrine: recommend an upgrade only when the\n  human names a limit the phone actually hit (e.g. low-light noise in an unfixable room), and name the cheapest\n  fix first (usually a $15–25 wired lav or moving the desk to the window).\n- **Consent:** anyone else appearing on camera (or audible) consents first — employees included. Filming in\n  public: know the local norms; recognizable strangers in a monetized video is a risk, not a vibe.\n- **Enhancement honesty:** AI touch-ups on a real human (descript Eye Contact, retouch, background replacement)\n  are edits of a real performance — fine, but stay within platform disclosure rules for altered content, and\n  never use them to fake a performance that didn't happen (e.g. syncing new words onto a face).\n- **Never shame a camera-shy human onto camera.** \"I don't want to film myself\" is a valid product requirement —\n  route to avatars or faceless formats without a lecture about authenticity.\n\n## Distinct from its siblings (route correctly)\n- **short-form-video-script** = writes the script (spoken + on-screen text + beats); **this** = delivers it to\n  camera. They pair on almost every shoot.\n- **scripting-and-storyboarding** = plans a multi-scene production (shot lists, storyboards); **this** = the\n  shoot-day performance craft for the human in front of the lens.\n- **heygen / synthesia** = synthetic presenters. Route there when the human can't or won't film, or when scale/\n  localization beats presence. They route *back here* for trust-led founder content — the hybrid (human films\n  the trust layer, avatar scales the informational layer) is the pro move.\n- **captions-and-clipping / capcut / descript** = post-production (captions, cuts, clips). descript's Eye\n  Contact AI patches a prompter read; it doesn't replace delivery.\n- **livestream-and-realtime** = live piece to camera — no retakes, different craft.\n- **ai-voiceover** = the voice without the face (one of the faceless routes).\n- **hook-writer** = the hook line itself; this skill delivers it fresh, first, every session.\n\n## Where this connects\n- **Reads first:** brand-profile + voice-builder (the person's off-camera voice is the on-camera target).\n- **Takes the script from:** short-form-video-script (short vertical) or youtube-long-form (long pieces); the\n  shoot plan from scripting-and-storyboarding; batch slots from batch-content-plan + content-calendar.\n- **Feeds:** captions-and-clipping / capcut / descript (the edit), opus-clip (clipping a long piece to camera\n  into shorts), cross-platform-repurposing (one performance, many placements),\n  social-proof-and-testimonials (founder-delivered trust content).\n- **Routes away:** heygen / synthesia (avatar instead of filming), ai-voiceover (faceless voice track).\n- **Publishes via:** the edited file → scheduling-and-queue → **WoopSocial.**\n- **Measure with:** native + analytics-and-reporting on 3s hold / AVD / completion — never fabricated.\n"
